Automated hex mesh generation has made significant advancements in recent decades. Historically, hex meshes have been produced using divide-and-conquer algorithms requiring node trianulation, which are notorious for poor topological neighborhood ranking. As a result, hex meshes often exhibit impractically low tets-per-hex-tetrahedra ratio. In this paper, a novel hex mesh traversal algorithm is presented, enabling efficient identification of extensive tetrahedral patterns within hex meshes with deep pyramidal structures. This new approach employs only basic data structures and is applicable to meshes containing irregular cell arrangements. Furthermore, hex mesh traversal is showcased as a solution to efficiently extract dense sub-meshes from larger volumes composed of numerous hex meshes, facilitating improved tets-per-hex-tetrahedra ratio of up to 58, thereby validating the existence of extensive tetrahedral patterns within hex meshes. The proposed traversal algorithm is instrumental in making hex mesh traversal-based tetrahedral pattern extraction methods more efficient. Consequently, hex mesh traversal-based methods can now be optimized with reduced running time of approximately one-tenth, thereby making them even more effective. Furthermore, the proposed algorithm is versatile and can be used in various contexts, including dense hex-to-tetrahedral conversion, hex mesh simplification, hex mesh splitting, and hex mesh validation. In addition, hex mesh traversal is also shown to be a suitable solution for tetrahedral pattern extraction during hex mesh validation, resulting in a substantial improvement of validation speed by two orders of magnitude, thereby making hex mesh traversal-based methods highly practical. The proposed algorithm is further substantiated by comprehensive numerical results, demonstrating its high practical value. The accompanying movie is available at: https://youtu.be/3y4H4t4oO6I. The source code is available upon request. \begin{figure}[!htbp