Natural language processing advancements have successfully applied single-language event extraction with impressive outcomes, but researchers have yet to explore the realm of multilingual event extraction, where a document in a different language may discuss the same event. To bridge this knowledge gap, a new multilingual event extraction dataset, the Multilingual Event Extraction dataset (MEE), has been created, comprising 7,892 event clusters across four languages: English, Spanish, Arabic, and Indonesian. These 7,922 event clusters are built around 2,538 events spanning news articles from ten news sources in four languages, totaling 35,671 annotated instances. Moreover, the dataset allows for the evaluation of three crucial research challenges in multilingual event extraction: identifying event clusters across languages, detecting language switches, and extracting event descriptions, including its applicability to zero-shot scenarios where there is no prior knowledge of the other languages involved. The MEE dataset is publicly accessible at this link: \url{https://github.com/monachy/MEE-data}. Researchers can use this dataset to advance the knowledge in multilingual event extraction, including the extraction of semantic information related to Arabic and Indonesian languages, which lack thorough research in this field. The potential outcomes of this research can lead to the development of innovative information extraction techniques, including those that focus on event aggregation and cross-language sentiment analysis. In addition to its primary function of evaluating multilingual event extraction, this dataset can be applied to a range of related tasks, such as detecting language switches and extracting event descriptions through the approach of weak supervision. The dataset is initially presented at the IJCNLP 2021 conference, where eight top-tier neural models submitted their results for a zero-shot setting, resulting in the selection of the best model for further research on this dataset. The potential outcomes of this research include gaining insights into multilingual event extraction, which has yet to be extensively explored in the field of natural language processing. } 

